WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.009
Film Courage: So you say writers don’t spend
enough time making sure that the idea that

00:00:04.009 --> 00:00:06.760
they’re putting out there is viable.

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:10.680
Wouldn’t you think that most writers are
choosing an idea that’s very personal to

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.299
them in some sense?

00:00:12.299 --> 00:00:16.550
Even if the story is not really about them,
it’s the core idea that affects them.

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:21.980
Why wouldn’t it translate if it’s so personal
and deep and rich to that writer, wouldn’t

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:27.800
putting something so deep and personal that
the writer is choosing to focus on be enough

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:28.800
to make the script viable?

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:29.910
Erik Bork, Screenwriter/Author: Well that’s
a great question.

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:33.670
First let me say when I talk about writers,
I’m talking about myself too because I’m

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:36.970
a writer and that’s still the main thing
I do and I make these things that they might

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:38.200
call mistakes.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.670
So it’s not me on high say “Oh, those
writers.

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:41.710
What idiots they are.”

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:44.230
It’s me saying we all naturally do this
stuff.

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:46.760
I just want to make that clear to your audience.&nbsp;

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:51.809
But that’s a great question and I’ve made
(I wouldn’t call it a mistake) but I’ve

00:00:51.809 --> 00:00:57.550
done this myself many times which is write
something that is personal to me that I think

00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:01.219
I felt something deeply about so audiences
will too.

00:01:01.219 --> 00:01:07.259
And one hopes they can pull that off and there
are some pitfalls in that approach that often

00:01:07.259 --> 00:01:13.990
happen one of them being we so internalize
what we went though, what we were thinking

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:19.719
and feeling and why it mattered so much in
our life to put it actually on the page in

00:01:19.719 --> 00:01:26.969
a way that audiences looking at a character
based on us, having experiences based on ours,

00:01:26.969 --> 00:01:31.399
for the audience to get those things that
are internal for us is harder than it looks.

00:01:31.399 --> 00:01:36.240
And often just because we felt it deeply doesn’t
mean we can write a character feeling that

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.499
same thing deeply and the audience will get
it.

00:01:39.499 --> 00:01:43.119
Sometimes they will, sometimes those are the
best movies, but a lot of times they won’t.

00:01:43.119 --> 00:01:47.149
And a lot of times that thing we went though
in our personal life that mattered so much

00:01:47.149 --> 00:01:53.889
to us doesn’t necessarily have the ingredients
of entertaining to watch story for an audience

00:01:53.889 --> 00:01:58.139
(like a movie or television audience) that
builds and complicates and gets worse in a

00:01:58.139 --> 00:02:01.169
really fun to watch way.&nbsp;

00:02:01.169 --> 00:02:04.959
Often when we’re writing real life and this
is any kind of true story (I’ve worked a

00:02:04.959 --> 00:02:08.489
lot on true stories) and people come to me
a lot with true stories because I worked on

00:02:08.489 --> 00:02:14.030
BAND OF BROTHERS, it’s kind of where I got
my start professionally.

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:21.860
What I really learned is that true stories
sometimes are really harder than fiction because

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:24.430
real life doesn’t have story structure typically.

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:29.569
Real life doesn’t have the things that we
writers have learned that audiences need to

00:02:29.569 --> 00:02:34.590
be really emotionally invested and entertainment
and to stay emotionally invested and entertained

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:38.819
over the course of an hour, half hour or two
hours, it just doesn’t and writers have

00:02:38.819 --> 00:02:46.090
to bring a lot of manipulation to a true story
to make it comprehensible to an audience,

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:51.450
to make it that kind of emotional journey
with that classic narrative build to it and

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:52.690
entertainment value to it.

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:56.410
It just doesn’t come naturally, we have
to impose it.

00:02:56.410 --> 00:03:01.269
So a lot of the things in our own life we
don’t have perspective on them, we can’t

00:03:01.269 --> 00:03:04.950
see them from that third-party person who
had a very different life from us.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:11.220
And how do we portray it on the scene to where
they will be really entertained and invested

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:13.300
in this character like us.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:18.629
And often we aren’t able to write that character
with enough perspective and enough clarity

00:03:18.629 --> 00:03:23.650
so that people will get what they’re going
through because to us it’s already so obvious,

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:26.580
it’s like a given, it’s inside of our
head, and getting it outside of our head and

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:30.549
getting it outside of our head and onto the
page in a way that others will really get

00:03:30.549 --> 00:03:33.719
it I think it harder than it looks.

00:03:33.719 --> 00:03:39.209
So when you’re identifying an idea for a
story, coming from a place that is passionate

00:03:39.209 --> 00:03:43.829
to you or that you’ve experienced is a good
start but it’s usually is not enough.

00:03:43.829 --> 00:03:48.060
You usually have to sort of test it yourself
by putting yourself in the shoes of a third

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:55.069
party anonymous viewer with a different life
story of your’s, how do I make this universal?

00:03:55.069 --> 00:04:00.219
Because our own pain and our own drama and
our own &nbsp;situations don’t necessarily have

00:04:00.219 --> 00:04:06.409
deep and compelling universally relatable
elements to them that anyone or millions of

00:04:06.409 --> 00:04:11.180
people would be able to instability grasp
and be emotionally invested.

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:14.890
I use that term a lot ‘emotionally investment’
because to me that’s the key thing we’re

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:15.890
always going for.

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:19.550
As an audience that’s the key thing we want,
we want to care.

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:25.650
And a lot of this focus on the idea is about
finding an idea that people (strangers, millions

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:30.009
of strangers) will have a chance of really
caring about this idea, caring about this

00:04:30.009 --> 00:04:32.780
character or these characters in this situation.

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:34.419
What makes them care?

00:04:34.419 --> 00:04:39.580
And so these seven elements a lot of it is
about me trying to put into words here are

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:45.780
the qualities I think are inherent in story
that can make an audience care.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:48.850
And when it’s coming from our own life experience,
a lot of times some of those elements can

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:51.219
be missing and we may not realize it.

